var RealProperty = {
  SearchTitle: "Real Property Assessment",
  SearchDescription: "State law mandates that all property is subject to taxation unless otherwise exempted. Your property taxes support necessary services provided to the residents of Los Angeles County. These include law enforcement, fire protection, education, parks and recreation, and other vital services. Property taxes are based on the assessed value of your property. Property tax bills show land and improvement values. Improvements include all assessable buildings and structures on the land. It does not necessarily mean that you have recently \u201Cimproved\u201D your property. In general, properties that are owned and used by educational, charitable, religious or government organizations may be exempt from certain property taxes. You may also qualify for certain exemptions.",
  PageTitle: "Real Property Assessment",
  PagesubTitle: "An Overview of Property Taxes",
  "1stParagraph": "State law mandates that all property is subject to taxation unless otherwise exempted. Your property taxes support necessary services provided to the residents of Los Angeles County. These include law enforcement, fire protection, education, parks and recreation, and other vital services. Property taxes are based on the assessed value of your property. Property tax bills show land and improvement values. Improvements include all assessable buildings and structures on the land. It does not necessarily mean that you have recently \u201Cimproved\u201D your property. In general, properties that are owned and used by educational, charitable, religious or government organizations may be exempt from certain property taxes. You may also qualify for certain exemptions.",
  Whatdoes: "What does the Assessor Do?",
  PrimaryResponsibilities: "The Assessor's Office has the following four primary responsibilities:",
  LocateTaxableProperty: "To locate all taxable property in the County and identify ownership.",
  EstablishValue: "To establish a taxable value for all property subject to property taxation.",
  CompleteAssessmentRoll: "To complete an assessment roll showing the assessed values of all property.",
  ApplyExemptions: "To apply all legal exemptions.",
  Prop13header: "Proposition 13",
  Prop13description: "In 1978, California voters passed Proposition 13, which substantially reduced property tax rates. As a result, the maximum levy cannot exceed 1% of a property\u2019s assessed value (plus bonded indebtedness and direct assessment taxes). Increases in assessed value are limited to 2% annually except when property changes of ownership or undergoes new construction.",
  Priorto1978: "Prior to 1978, real property was appraised cyclically, with no more than a five-year interval between reassessments. Since property values were systematically reviewed and updated, assessed values were usually kept at or near current market value levels. In contrast, under Proposition 13, properties are reassessed to current market value only upon <u>a change in ownership or completion of new construction</u> (called the base year value).",
  Essentially: "Essentially, Proposition 13 converted the market value-based property tax system to an acquisition value-based system.",
  Prop13item1: "A change in ownership;",
  Prop13item2: "Completed new construction;",
  Prop13item3: "New construction partially completed on the lien date (January 1); or",
  Prop13item4: "A decline-in-value (see Market Value Decline - Prop. 8).",
  ChangeOwnershipheader: "Change in Ownership Appraisals",
  ChangeOwnershiptext1: "When a publicly recorded transfer occurs, the Assessor receives a copy of the deed and determines whether a reappraisal is required under State law. The date of reappraisal is generally the recording date of the deed that transfers ownership. However, the reappraisal of property acquired by inheritance from an estate or living trust occurs as of the date of death of the property owner, not as of the date of distribution to the beneficiary. If required, an appraisal is made to determine the new market value of the property.",
  ChangeOwnershiptext2: "Upon notification of the new assessment, the property owner has the right to appeal the value if he/she does not agree with it.",
  ChangeOwnershiptext3: 'The transfer of property between husband and wife, and registered domestic partners does not cause a reappraisal for property tax purposes. This includes transfers resulting from death, divorce, or termination of domestic partnership. Also, the addition of joint tenants, whether related or not, does not result in a reappraisal. In most cases, transfers by irrevocable trusts are reappraisable. There are several exclusions available for certain qualified situations. Please see the section titled "Tax Savings Programs".',
  NewConstheader: "New Construction Appraisals",
  NewConsttext1: "Copies of building permits are also sent to the Assessor. New buildings, additions, and other structures require an appraisal. Structural repairs and/or replacement are not appraisable in most situations.",
  NewConsttext2: "The value of new construction is added to the existing improvement assessed value. The new assessed value will not change except for the annual inflation adjustment of up to 2%. As with all newly assessed values, the property owner has the right to appeal the value.",
  Supplementalheader: "Supplemental Assessments - SB 813",
  Supplementaltext1: "State law requires the Assessor to reappraise property upon change in ownership or completion of new construction. The supplemental assessment reflects the difference between the new value and the old value. The Auditor-Controller calculates the supplemental property tax, and prorates it, based upon the number of months remaining in the fiscal year in which the event occurred. The fiscal year runs from July 1 through June 30.",
  Supplementaltext2: "A change in ownership or new construction completion which occurs between January 1 and May 31 results in two supplemental assessments and two supplemental tax bills. The first supplemental bill is for the remainder of the fiscal year in which the event occurred. The second supplemental bill is for the subsequent fiscal year.",
  Supplementaltext3: "Notices of Assessed Value Change are mailed to property owners before supplemental tax bills are issued.",
  Supplementaltext4: "Remember that supplemental tax bills are in addition to the regular annual tax bills. Supplemental bills go directly to the property owner, and not to an impound account - where one might exist.",
  Supplementaltext5: "Click the button below for an estimate of your supplemental taxes resulting from a change in ownership,"
};
var ChangeinOwnership = {
  SearchTitle: "Change In Ownership",
  SearchDescription: "All properties in Los Angeles County are required to be reassessed as of the date a Change in Ownership occurs.\u202F In some situations, exclusions from reassessment may apply, therefore avoiding an increase in your property taxes.",
  PageTitle: "Change in Ownership",
  allproperties1: "All properties in Los Angeles County are required to be reassessed as of the date a Change in Ownership occurs.\u202F In some situations, exclusions from reassessment may apply, therefore avoiding an increase in your property taxes.",
  allproperties2: "The Assessor\u2019s office reviews all recorded deeds to determine which properties require reappraisal under the Revenue and Taxation Code (R&T Code). Our Ownership Division within the Assessor\u2019s Office may also discover changes in ownership through other means, such as property owners self-reporting, through the State Board of equalization, or trade publications and public filings.",
  ChangesandNotification: "Changes in Ownership and Notification to the Assessor",
  ChangesandNotification1: "All changes in ownership require that property owners notify their Assessor as soon as possible to avoid potential interest charges or other penalties.  A transfer can be a sale or purchase, but it also can be a gift or inheritance. Payment or consideration for the property is not required.",
  ChangesandNotification2: "The statute of limitations under California Revenue and Taxation Code,  provides that the Assessor must reassess as many as eight (8) prior assessment years due to failure to file the ",
  ChangesandNotification2bold: "Change in Ownership  Statement (COS form).",
  Ownersofa: 'Owners of a property that has experienced a change in ownership (except by death of an owner) must file a <strong>Preliminary Change of Ownership Report (PCOR)</strong> when the transfer is recorded. Or if un-recorded, a <strong>Change in Ownership Statement (COS)</strong> within the prescribed time limits from the date of transfer. Failure to notify the Assessor may result in the assessment of penalties. These forms are available by clicking on the "Forms" tab above.',
  ChangesandNotification3cont: "when the transfer is recorded. Or if un-recorded, a Change in Ownership Statement within the prescribed time limits from the date of transfer. Failure to notify the Assessor may result in the assessment of penalties.",
  ChangesandNotification4: "If the change in ownership was the result of a death, the law requires that the ",
  ChangesandNotification4bold: "Death of Real Property Owner ",
  ChangesandNotification4cont: "form be filed with the Assessor within 150 days from the date of death.",
  Note: "<b>NOTE</b>: If the change in ownership was the result of a death, the law requires that the Death of Real Property Form <strong>(BOD-502-D /ASSR-176)</strong> be filed with the Assessor within 150 days from the date of death.",
  Exclusions: "Exclusions",
  Exclusionstext: "While a transfer of property may constitute a change in ownership, a number of exclusions exist so that certain types of transfers are excluded, by law, from the definition of change in ownership. For the following types of transfers, the property will not be reappraised. ",
  ChangesClaim: "Changes that require a claim to avoid reassessment",
  ChangeswithClaim: "Changes in ownership that require a\u202Fclaim to be filed\u202Fto avoid reassessment include the following: ",
  ChangeswithClaim1: "Proposition 19: Intergenerational Transfers Parent to Child & Grandparent to Grandchild*:",
  ChangeswithClaim1text: "Transfers of the principal place of residence between parents and their children and from grandparents to their grandchildren All other inter family transfers except those mentioned above do not qualify for any reassessment exclusion. (Examples: brother to sister, uncle to niece, cousin to cousin, etc.)",
  ChangeswithClaim2: "Proposition 19: 55 & Older Transfer Assessment of Principal Residence*:",
  ChangeswithClaim2text: "The purchase of a replacement dwelling by a person who is 55 years of age or older, where the replacement dwelling will be that person\u2019s principal place of residence. In such cases, the base year value of the previous home may be transferred to the new home so that the new home will not be reassessed to its current fair market value.\xA0",
  ChangeswithClaim3: "Proposition 19:  Severely Disabled*:",
  ChangeswithClaim3text: "The purchase of a new principal residence by a person who is severely disabled (physical disability or impairment only \u2013 not mentally). ",
  ChangeswithClaimNote: "*NOTE: ",
  ChangeswithClaimNotetext: "In November 2020,\u202FCalifornia voters passed Proposition 19, which makes changes to property tax benefits for families (effective February 16, 2021). Please visit our\u202FProposition 19 resource page\u202Ffor more information and important filing requirements.",
  Prop19Clickhere: "Learn More about Prop. 19",
  ChangeswithClaim4: "Transfers of the principal residence between two co-tenants that occur upon the death of one of the co-tenants, provided that: ",
  ChangeswithClaim4item1: "The two co-tenants together owned 100 percent of the property as tenants in common or joint tenants. ",
  ChangeswithClaim4item2: "The two co-tenants must be owners of record for the one-year period immediately preceding the death of one of the co-tenants. ",
  ChangeswithClaim4item3: "The property must have been the principal residence of both co-tenants for the one-year period immediately preceding the death of one of the co-tenants. ",
  ChangeswithClaim4item4: "The surviving co-tenant must obtain a 100 percent interest in the property. ",
  ChangeswithClaim4item5: "The surviving co-tenant must sign an affidavit affirming that he or she continuously resided at the residence for the one-year period preceding the decedent co-tenant\u2019s date of death. ",
  ChangeswithClaim5: "Transfers of real property between registered domestic partners that occurred between January 1, 2000 and January 1, 2006 (section 62(p) of the Revenue and Taxation Code). County assessors are required to reverse any reassessments that resulted from any transfers of real property between registered domestic partners that occurred during this time period if the property owner files a timely claim. However, relief for such a reversal is applied only on a prospective basis. The registered domestic partners will not receive any refunds. ",
  ChangeswithClaim6: "The purchase of a replacement property if the original property was taken by governmental action, such as eminent domain or inverse condemnation. ",
  ChangesExclv2: "Changes excluded from reassessment",
  ChangesExcludedv3: "Changes in ownership that qualify for reassessment exclusions (but may require supportive documentation from the property owners) include the following: ",
  ChangesExcludeditem1: "Transfers of real property between husband and wife, which include transfers in and out of a trust for the benefit of a spouse, the addition of a spouse on a deed, transfers upon the death of a spouse, and transfers pursuant to a divorce settlement or court order (section 63 of the Revenue and Taxation Code; Rule 462.220). ",
  ChangesExcludeditem2: "Transfers of real property between registered domestic partners that occur on or after January 1, 2006, which include transfers in and out of a trust for the benefit of a partner, the addition of a partner on a deed, transfers upon the death of a partner, and transfers pursuant to a settlement agreement or court order upon termination of the domestic partnership (section 62(p) of the Revenue and Taxation Code). ",
  ChangesExcludeditem3: "Transactions only to correct the name(s) of the person(s) holding title to real property or transfers of real property for the purpose of perfecting title to the property (for example, a name change upon marriage). ",
  ChangesExcludeditem4: "Transfers of real property between co-owners that result in a change in the method of holding title to the property without changing the proportional interests of the co-owners, such as a partition of a tenancy in common. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem5: "Transfers between an individual or individuals and a legal entity or between legal entities, such as a co-tenancy to a partnership, or a partnership to a corporation, that results solely in a change in the method of holding title to the real property and in which proportional ownership interests of the transferors and the transferees, whether represented by stock, partnership interest, or otherwise, in each and every piece of real property transferred, remains the same after the transfer. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem6: "The creation, assignment, termination, or reconveyance of a lender\u2019s security interest in real property or any transfer required for financing purposes only (for example, co-signor). It is highly advisable that the title be reverted back to its original owner(s) within 6 months of recordation. Lack of or failure to return title as stated is a potential for change in ownership. In such case, the Assessor may reassess the property.",
  ChangesExcludeditem6note: "Note: The Assessor may request financial substantiation for the transaction once title has been reverted back to the original owner(s).",
  ChangesExcludeditem7: "The substitution of a trustee of a trust or mortgage. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem8: "Transfers that result in the creation of a joint tenancy in which the transferor remains as one of the joint tenants. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem9: "Transfers of joint tenancy property to return the property to the person who created a joint tenancy (i.e., the original transferor). ",
  ChangesExcludeditem10: "Transfers of real property to a revocable trust, where the transferor retains the power to revoke the trust or where the trust is created for the benefit of the transferor or the transferor\u2019s spouse. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem11: "Transfers of real property into a trust that may be revoked by the creator/grantor who is also a joint tenant, and which names the other joint tenant(s) as beneficiaries when the creator/grantor dies. ",
  ChangesExcludeditem12: "Transfers of real property to an irrevocable trust for the benefit of the creator/grantor or the creator/grantor\u2019s spouse. "
};
var ChangeinAddress = {
  SearchTitle: "Change of Mailing Address",
  SearchDescription: "Checklist of documents to submit",
  PageTitle: "Change of Mailing Address",
  Itis: "It is the property owner\u2019s responsibility to report a change of mailing address.\u202F Please click on the \u201CForms\u201D Tab above to print out a change of mailing address application.",
  Sendall: "Send all mailing address change requests to",
  Formore: "For more assistance, please contact the Ownership Services Division by telephone at  ",
  oremailat: "or email at ",
  Formore2: 'For more information or additional help, please use our <a href="/contact/assessor/2">Contact Form</a>.',
  Important: '<u>IMPORTANT</u>: A checklist of documents or items an individual must submit with the Change of Mailing Address form is available on the "FAQ" tab above. <u>Please review this list of required documents before sending the Change of Mailing Address form</u>. Insufficient supporting documentation will cause delays in the processing of your change request.',
  ImportantAlert: "<strong>IMPORTANT:</strong> The security of property owner information is of paramount importance to the Assessor\u2019s Office.<br> To ensure that change of mailing address requests received by the Assessor\u2019s Office are being processed pursuant to the actual property owners\u2019 wishes, in certain circumstances, additional documentation may be required to complete the processing of a request. We have included information on the supporting documentation that should be submitted when requesting a change of mailing address for properties held by individuals, in trust, by a legal entity, and in a deceased property owner\u2019s estate. Click the \u201CFAQ\u201D tab below to view the available checklist. <u>Please be sure to include the appropriate supporting documentation when submitting your Change of Mailing Address request form, as lack of sufficient supporting documentation will cause delays in processing.</u>",
  ItisImportant: "It is important for the Assessor\u2019s Office to maintain accurate records regarding property mailing addresses, because the mailing address of record for a particular property is where property tax bills and other important property tax notices for that property will be sent. If a mailing address has changed, it is the property owner\u2019s responsibility to notify the Assessor\u2019s Office, so we can make any necessary changes to our records. Such changes can be reported by completing and submitting a Change of Mailing Address (<strong>ASSR-451</strong>) request form to the Assessor\u2019s Office. The request form is available under the \u201CFORMS\u201D tab above."
};
var NewConstruction = {
  SearchTitle: "New Construction, Remodeling, Repair",
  SearchDescription: "The Los Angeles County Assessor\u2019s Office reviews permit information received from local permitting jurisdictions for  construction, remodeling or repair, individually, to determine the appraisability of the work permitted. Described below is a guide to what is and is not assessable by our office.",
  PageTitle: "New Construction, Remodeling, Repair",
  Description: "The Los Angeles County Assessor\u2019s Office reviews permit information received from local permitting jurisdictions for  construction, remodeling or repair, individually, to determine the appraisability of the work permitted. Described below is a guide to what is and is not assessable by our office. ",
  NewConst: "New Construction",
  UnderCali: "Under California property tax law, \u201Cnew construction\u201D is defined in four general categories: ",
  newconst: "new construction",
  isdefined: "is defined in four general categories:",
  category1: "Any substantial addition to land or improvements, including fixtures. ",
  category2: "Any physical alteration of any improvement, or a portion thereof, to a \u201Clike-new\u201D condition, or to extend its economic life, or to change the way in which the improvement, or portion thereof, is used (change of use). ",
  category3: "Any substantial physical alteration of land which constitutes a major rehabilitation of the land or changes the manner in which it is used (grading and land tailoring for example). ",
  category4: "Any substantial physical rehabilitation, renovation or modernization of any fixture that converts it to the substantial equivalent of a new fixture or any substitution of a new fixture. ",
  NewAdditions: "New Additions",
  Worktoa: "Work to a property that consists of erecting a new building, increasing the square footage of a property, or adding improvements to a home that didn\u2019t previously exist are all considered assessable.",
  Examples: "Examples of such new construction work would include: ",
  Examples1: "Adding an additional floor(s) or levels, including subterranean levels below ground ",
  Examples2: "Additions to existing floors such as room additions, garage additions, additional living space. ",
  Examples3: "Converting an unfinished basement or attic into living space ",
  Examples4: "Adding a new bathroom ",
  Examples5: "Adding a garage ",
  Examples6: "Adding an elevator ",
  Examples7: "Adding an Accessory Dwelling Unit or Junior Accessory Dwelling Unit (attached or detached)",
  RemodelingandRepair: "Remodeling and Repair",
  Whatis: "What is and isn\u2019t considered assessable for remodeling projects can be confusing to property owners. If the projects are part of normal maintenance and repair, such as termite, dry rot repairs or roof replacements, then they are not considered assessable. This also extends to projects that are primarily cosmetic like replacing bathroom or kitchen fixtures of similar quality.  ",
  Anyconstruction: "Any construction project that raises the quality class or extends the useable life of the property are considered assessable. These \u201Clike new\u201D or substantially equivalent to new renovations involve more substantial structural changes. They include projects that change the layout of the kitchen or bathroom, alter the usage of current property, or upgrade the capacity of the plumbing or electrical systems. Below are some examples but it is important to keep in mind that each project and property is unique, so the Assessor reviews each permit and property project individually. ",
  Assessable: "Assessable",
  Assessable1: "Bathroom remodel - the addition of one, or structural changes, upgrading of plumbing and/or electrical systems, changing the floor plan, increasing the size, replacing cabinets, countertops, flooring or fixtures with upgraded material and finishes ",
  Assessable2: "Kitchen - structural changes, upgrading of plumbing and/or electrical systems, changing the floor plan, increasing the size, replacing cabinets, countertops, flooring or built-in appliances with upgraded material and finishes ",
  Assessable3: "Completely renovating a property or significant portion (torn down to studs, few walls remaining, major rehab) ",
  Assessable4: "Change of use (converting a property from industrial to residential use or retail to residential) ",
  Assessable5v3: "Upgrading electrical panel capacity from 100 amps to 200 amps",
  NotAssessable: "Not Assessable",
  NotAssessable1: "Bath/Kitchen remodel - Replacement of cabinets, counter tops, flooring, fixtures or built-in appliances with items of similar quality ",
  NotAssessable2: "Dry rot or termite repairs ",
  NotAssessable3: "Re-roofing ",
  NotAssessable4: "Replacing or repairing doors, windows, fences or decks ",
  Exclusions: "Exclusions",
  Someconstruction: "Some construction and remodeling may be eligible for exclusion from reassessment such as fire damage or seismic retrofitting and some solar installations. Additionally, some new construction that makes a primary residence accessible for the disabled may be excluded. If you are filing a request for property tax exclusion for modifications made to improve accessibility, in addition to the claim and proof of disability, a statement must be provided that identifies the construction, installation, or modification that was necessary to make the structure more accessible.",
  Seeforms: "See \u201CForms\u201D tab above and click for required forms."
};
var NewHomeowners = {
  SearchTitle: "New Homeowners",
  SearchDescription: "We work with real estate professionals throughout Los Angeles County to help make this a positive experience and to aid you in avoiding some of the common pitfalls that may occur in the property tax process.",
  PageTitle: "New Homeowners",
  description: "Congratulations on your purchase of a home! We work with real estate professionals throughout Los Angeles County to help make this a positive experience and to aid you in avoiding some of the common pitfalls that may occur in the property tax process. It is important that you take a few minutes to review the following:",
  homeex: "Homeowners' Exemption",
  homeexdesc: "If you own and occupy your home as your principal place of residence, you are eligible for a Homeowners\u2019 Exemption that reduces your property tax by about $70 annually. You will be sent an application about three to four months after your deed is recorded.",
  homeexbutton: "Homeowners' Exemption",
  annualproptax: "Annual Property Taxes",
  annualproptaxdesc: "Annual tax bills are mailed once a year by November 1. The first installment is delinquent if payment is not received on or postmarked by December 10, and the second installment is delinquent if payment is not received on or postmarked by April 10.",
  annualproptaxdescbold: '\u202FIf you do not receive your tax bill by November 1, you may request a <a href="https://ttc.lacounty.gov/request-duplicate-bill/" target="_blank">Duplicate Bill</a> or call the Treasurer and Tax Collector at 213-974-2111.',
  annualproptaxdesc2: "Even though known taxes are prorated between the buyer and seller during escrow and proper credit is given to each, all annual property taxes may not have been paid to the Tax Collector at that time. By calling the property tax information number at <b>1 (888) 807-2111</b>, you can determine if any annual taxes are due and how to obtain a substitute tax bill if necessary.\u202F",
  annualproptaxdescbold2: "State law makes it your responsibility to pay the taxes whether or not you receive a bill.",
  supproptax: "Supplemental Property Taxes",
  supproptaxdesc: "In addition to annual taxes, you may be responsible for paying supplemental property taxes. Supplemental bills are based on the difference between the prior assessed value and your new assessed value, which is generally your purchase price. This amount is prorated based on the number of months left in the fiscal year from the date you purchased your home. If the property is reassessed at a lower value, you will receive a refund. ",
  supproptaxdesc2: "Supplemental tax bills are your responsibility and will be mailed directly to you by the Treasurer and Tax Collector\u2019s Office approximately 6 months after your purchase. These bills are not generally paid by impound accounts. You may view your estimated supplemental tax amount by clicking the button below. ",
  supproptaxdesc2notaxestimator: "Supplemental tax bills are your responsibility and will be mailed directly to you by the Treasurer and Tax Collector\u2019s Office approximately 6 months after your purchase. These bills are not generally paid by impound accounts.",
  supproptaxbutton: "Supplemental Tax Estimator",
  transferexc: "Transfer Reassessment Exclusions Proposition 19",
  transferexcdesc: "Your home may be excluded from reassessment if the transfer of property was between parent(s) and child(ren), or from grandparent(s) to grandchild(ren). Also, if you are disabled, or a senior age 55 or older, you may qualify to transfer the assessed value from your original property to your replacement property. Contact our staff or click the button to download the Homeowners' Guide to Proposition 58 and Proposition 193. ",
  prop19button: "Proposition 19",
  assistancev2: 'For more information or additional help, please use our <a href="/contact/assessor">Contact Form</a>. '
};
var hox = {
  SearchTitle: "Homeowners' Exemption",
  SearchDescription: "If you own a home and it is your principal place of residence on January 1, you may apply for an exemption of $7,000 from your assessed value. New property owners will automatically receive a Claim For Homeowners' Property Tax Exemption. Homeowners\u2019 Exemptions may also apply to a supplemental assessment if the prior owner did not claim the exemption.",
  PageTitle: "Homeowners' Exemption",
  Textv2: "If you own a home and it is your principal place of residence on January 1, you may apply for an exemption of $7,000 from your assessed value. New property owners will automatically receive a Homeowners' Property Tax Exemption Claim Form <b>(BOE-266/ASSR-515)</b>. Homeowners\u2019 Exemptions may also apply to a supplemental assessment if the prior owner did not claim the exemption.",
  Further: "Further instructions are included with the claim form which is available by <u>clicking on the \u201CForms\u201D tab above.</u>",
  Ifeligible: "If you are eligible and would like to apply for the exemption, download, complete, sign, and return form BOE-266/ASSR-515 claim form by mail to our office address below:",
  Forthesecurity: "For the security of your data we do not accept electronically transmitted documents with sensitive personal information. Follow the instructions on the form and complete the required information.",
  Formoreinfo: 'For more information or additional help, please use our <a href="/contact/assessor/6">Contact Form</a>. One of our Teams will provide you additional instructions via email or can confirm receipt of a previously mailed claim.',
  Call: "Call",
  "213bold": "(213)974-3211",
  or: "or",
  "888bold": "1(888)807-2111",
  Forforms: "for forms and additional information.",
  Completedforms: "Completed forms and any documentation can be emailed to:",
  Youcanalsoreach: "You can also call and reach our Exemptions staff at: <strong>(213) 974-3415</strong> for forms and additional information.",
  Versusv2: "Homeowners' Exemption versus Homestead Protection",
  Statutory: "<strong>Homeowners\u2019 Exemption</strong> is a statutory tax provision, which provides a homeowner a $7,000 reduction off the taxable value on their primary residence. With an approximate 1% property tax rate, the exemption provides roughly a $70 annual saving off your property taxes.",
  HomesteadExemptionv2: '<strong>Homestead Protection</strong> is a civil code provision, which may protect part of the homes equity from creditors. It has no application in property assessment or taxation. For more information on automatic and declaring a Homestead Protection, please visit Department of Consumer & Business Affairs\u2019 website on <a href="https://dcba.lacounty.gov/portfolio/homestead-protection/" target="_blank">Homestead Protection</a>.',
  FilingInfo: `For more information on filing, please visit the Los Angeles County Registrar Recorder\u2019s <a href="https://www.lavote.gov/home/recorder/property-document-recording/forms/homesteads" target="_blank">Homestead Exemption</a> page for forms and filing requirements. They can also be reached at <strong>1 (800) 201-8999</strong>. The Registrar Recorder's Office is the authorized county agency to record deeds and Declarations of Homestead.`,
  Ifeligible2: "If you are eligible and would like to apply, the Homeowners\u2019 Property Tax Exemption Claim form can be sent by mail or submitted online. Follow instructions as supporting documentation may be required.",
  Disclaimer: "<strong>Disclaimer: Online Submission of Homeowner's Exemption (HOX) in Spanish</strong><br>Please note that the Los Angeles County e-File service does not currently support the online submission of the Homeowner's Exemption (HOX) form in Spanish. If you wish to submit your HOX application in Spanish, you must download the Spanish version of the form from our website and mail it directly to the following address:",
  Weapologize: `We apologize for any inconvenience and appreciate your understanding. For assistance, please contact the <a href="https://assessor.lacounty.gov/contact/assessor/6" target="_blank">Assessor's Office</a>.`
};
var ContestAssessment = {
  SearchTitle: "Contesting Your Assessed Value",
  SearchDescription: "If you are contesting a supplemental bill for a change of ownership or new construction, please contact your regional Assessor's office for a review and explanation of the assessed value change. Be aware that to preserve your rights to a formal appeal with the Assessment Appeals Board, you must file an application with the Assessment Appeals Board within 60 days of the mailing date of your notice of assessed value change or tax bill. The filing of an assessment appeal is separate from the informal review the Assessor may undertake at your request.",
  PageTitle: "Contesting Your Assessed Value",
  Therearetwo: "There are two ways to contest your assessed value.",
  WiththeAssessor: "With the Assessor",
  WiththeAssessor1: "If you are contesting a supplemental bill for a change of ownership or new construction, please contact your regional Assessor's Office for a review and explanation of the assessed value change. Be aware that to preserve your rights to a formal appeal with the Assessment Appeals Board, you must file an application with the Assessment Appeals Board within 60 days of the mailing date of your notice of assessed value change or tax bill.  The filing of an assessment appeal is separate from the informal review the Assessor may undertake at your request.",
  WiththeAssessor2: "If you are contesting your property\u2019s annual tax bill, you may request an informal review within the Assessor\u2019s Office called a \u201CDecline-in-Value\u201D review. Be aware that to preserve your rights to a formal appeal with the Assessment Appeals Board, you must file an application with the Assessment Appeals Board during the open filing period beginning on July 2 and ending on November 30 of the year in question. The filing of an assessment appeal is separate from the informal review the Assessor may undertake at your request. ",
  WiththeBoard: "With the Assessment Appeals Board ",
  WiththeBoard1: "Property owners have the right to file a formal appeal with the Assessment Appeals Board, which acts as an impartial third party in disputes between the property owner and Assessor. Under authority of the California State constitution, the Board of Supervisors of Los Angeles county created the Assessment Appeals Board to sit as the Board of Equalization for the County of Los Angeles.",
  Decline: "Decline-in-Value: Reviewing your value informally with the Assessor's Office",
  ifyou: "If you believe that your property value warrants review due to a decline in market value, you may file an application for Decline-in-Value Review with the Assessor\u2019s Office.",
  The2022: "The 2022 Decline-in-Value filing period is July 2, 2022 through November 30, 2022. ",
  Applicationswill2022: "Applications will be available beginning on July 2, 2022. ",
  Clickon: "Click on \u201CForms\u201D tab above for the Decline In Value application (available during the filing period between July 2 through December 1) ",
  CaliforniaProp13: "California\u2019s Proposition 13 established the base year value for property tax assessment. It also caps the growth of a property\u2019s assessed value at no more than two percent a year unless the market value of a property falls below the trended base year value. ",
  in1978: "In 1978, California voters passed Proposition 8, a constitutional amendment that allows a temporary reduction in assessed value when a property suffers a \u201Cdecline-in-value.\u201D A decline-in-value occurs when the ",
  Currentmarket: "current market value of your property is less than the current assessed value as of January 1 of a given year.",
  TheOffice2: "The Assessor's Office proactively reviewed hundreds of thousands of properties in past years, that may have declined in value due to the housing market downturn. Now that property values are rising, property owners that received a reduction in property taxes could see their property tax bill increase by more than two percent. Property owners are encouraged to review the FAQ page by clicking on the \u201CFAQ\u201D tab above for more information about Decline-in-Value and how property values are assessed. ",
  Aboutthisprocess: "About this Process",
  AppealinganAssessment: "Appealing an Assessment with the Assessment Appeals Board ",
  toappeal1: "To protect your right to appeal the value of your property you may file a formal appeal with the ",
  assessmentappeals: "Assessment Appeals Board",
  toappeal2: "For Supplemental, Adjusted Supplemental, Adjusted Property Tax Bills, or Escaped Assessments, a formal appeal may be filed within 60 days of either:",
  mailingdate1: "the mailing date printed on a Notice of Assessed Value Change",
  mailingdate2: "the date of mailing printed on the tax bill, or",
  postmarkdate: "the postmark date for the tax bill, whichever is later",
  regularroll: 'For the "Regular Assessment Roll," a formal appeal may be filed from July 2 through November 30. If November 30 falls on a Saturday, Sunday, or a legal holiday, an application is valid if either filed or postmarked by the next business day.',
  Appealsmustbe: "Appeals must be filed with the Assessment Appeals Board, Room B-4, Kenneth Hahn Hall of Administration, 500 W. Temple Street, Los Angeles, CA 90012-2770. Click the button below to visit the Assessment Appeals Board website for more information and to file your appeal online, or call <strong>(213) 974-1471</strong> to request an application by phone."
};
