var PossessoryInterest = {
  SearchTitle: "Possessory Interest",
  SearchDescription: "Possessory interests are interests in real property that exist when a person or entity leases, rents or uses real estate owned by a government agency for its exclusive use under any form of agreement or permit. Some of these Possessory Interests are Taxable. If the Possessory Interest that is created meets the legal standards for Possession and a private benefit, Independence, Durability and Exclusivity then the Possessory Interest will be taxable. The taxation of this interest is similar to the taxation of owners of privately owned property. However, the holder of a possessory interest is taxed only on the interest in the property created by the agreement and use, not the property\u2019s full value in perpetuity as all other Fee property. ",
  PageTitle: "Possessory Interest",
  PrintableGuide: "Printable Guide",
  Guideto: "Guide to Taxable Possessory Interest for Publicly Owned Real Property",
  Download: "Download",
  Whatis: "What is Taxable Possessory Interest (PI)?",
  Whenaperson: "Possessory interests are interests in real property that exist when a person or entity leases, rents or uses real estate owned by a government agency for its exclusive use under any form of agreement or permit. Some of these Possessory Interests are Taxable. If the Possessory Interest that is created meets the legal standards for Possession and a private benefit, Independence, Durability and Exclusivity then the Possessory Interest will be taxable. The taxation of this interest is similar to the taxation of owners of privately owned property. However, the holder of a possessory interest is taxed only on the interest in the property created by the agreement and use, not the property\u2019s full value in perpetuity as all other Fee property. ",
  Examplesof: "Examples of Possessory Interest",
  TaxablePIs: "Taxable PI's are created by almost any use of government-owned real property including the following examples.",
  Boatslips: "Boat slips on public lakes, rivers, or ocean marinas",
  Ministorage: "Mini-storage facilities under freeways",
  Privatewalkways: "Private walkways above city streets",
  Airplane: "Airplane tie-downs and hangars at county airports",
  Cattlegrazing: "Cattle grazing rights on government land",
  Privateentities: "Private entities leasing government-owned buildings",
  Concessionspaces: "Concession spaces at convention centers and fairgrounds",
  Cabinsand: "Cabins and campgrounds on U.S. Forestry-owned lands",
  Skiresorts: "Ski resorts on public lands",
  Proshops: "Pro shops or golf operators at a public golf course",
  Terminaland: "Terminal and cargo spaces at airports",
  Containeroperations: "Container operations at harbors",
  Cabletelevision: "Cable television right-of-way easements",
  Retailbusiness: "Retail business operations in a publicly-owned building",
  Rentalcar: "Rental car facilities at airports",
  Farmingon: "Farming on community college district land",
  Miningoperations: "Mining operations on public land",
  Privatelyowned: "Privately owned medical clinics operating in a county hospital",
  Adultnight: "Adult/night schools operating in a public school",
  Christmastree: "Christmas tree lots on a freeway underpass",
  Stables: "Stables on river annex",
  Concertorair: "Concert or air show on public land",
  Employeehousing: "Employee housing on tax-exempt land",
  ValuationMethod: "Valuation Method",
  Whenanew: "When a new base year value or any value (New Construction, Decline in value or Rule 21 valuation etc.) is established for a possessory interest property, the Assessor can use all approaches to value. The same valuation methodology as Fee property will apply (the income, comparative sales, or cost approach). However, the value will only be calculated based on the appropriate term of possession as opposed to in perpetuity. The quality and quantity of the available market information, the type of interest being valued, and the property type will determine which of the three valuation approaches is most appropriate to use. For more detailed information refer to Board of Equalization Property Tax Rules 20, 21 and 22, as well as Revenue & Taxation Code Section 107.",
  IncomeApproach: "Income Approach",
  Thisisthe: "This is the most commonly used method for valuing a possessory interest. Using this approach, the PI value is estimated by first determining the net income. The net income results from subtracting the anticipated vacancy rate, collection loss, and management expenses from the economic income. The net income is then multiplied by a present worth factor to arrive at the PI value.",
  Usingthe: "Using the economic net income for the term of possession allows the Assessor to value only the rights \u201Cpossessed\u201D by the tenant and exclude any non-taxable rights retained by the government landlord.",
  ComparativeSales: "Comparative Sales Approach",
  Inthisapproach: "In this approach to value, the sales price of the property or similar possessory interest properties is used to determine possessory interest value. Rent paid on the property and any other obligations assumed by the buyer are valued at present worth and added to the sales price.",
  Costapproach: "Cost Approach",
  Inthecost: "In the cost approach, the land and improvement values are determined separately. The land value is determined using the comparative sales approach or the income approach. Consideration is given for the reversionary value of the land at the end of the anticipated term of possession. The improvement value is estimated by subtracting the accrued depreciation from the replacement cost.",
  Considering: "Consideration is given for the estimated value of the improvements at the end of the anticipated term of possession. The total value of the PI is determined by adding the estimated land value to the estimated improvement value.",
  PossessoryInterestPropTax: "Possessory Interest Property Tax",
  Possessoryinterest: "Possessory interest tax helps pay the holders fair share of services and benefits that owners of similar taxable properties enjoy. These services and benefits include fire and police protection, schools, and local government.",
  Thepersonor: "The person or entity in possession of the property on the lien date (January 1) is liable for the entire subsequent fiscal year\u2019s taxes. Unfortunately, no provision is made for the Assessor to prorate the taxes if the possessory interest is terminated after the lien date.",
  AssessorResponsibility: "Assessor's Responsibility",
  Bylaw: "By law, every governmental agency in the county must respond to the Assessor\u2019s annual request for information. The information assists the Assessor in conducting fair and accurate possessory interest assessments.",
  Valuing: "Valuing Taxable Possessory Interest",
  Abaseyear: "A base year value is established for taxable possessory interests upon a change in ownership or completion of new construction under the guidelines of Proposition 13. This value, by law, will only increase by a maximum of 2% per year, until a new reappraiseable event (change of ownership or completion of new construction) occurs, or the property suffers a decline-in-value. Furthermore, BOE Rule 21 dictates that Possessory Interest Assessments be reviewed as the term declines to determine if the assessment should be reduced as the interest in the property diminishes. The valuation of possessory interests is different from other forms of property tax appraisal in a few ways: ",
  Thevaluation: "The valuation of possessory interests is different from other forms of property tax appraisal in two ways:",
  Onlytherights: "Only the rights held by the private user are valued.",
  TheAssessormust: "The Assessor must not include the value of the lessor\u2019s retained rights in the property (the \u201Creversionary interest\u201D).",
  Asaresult: "As a result, possessory interest assessments are frequently less than the assessments of similar privately owned property as they do not include the Government\u2019s retained rights or the \u201Creversionary interest\u201D. ",
  Foranexpanded: "For an expanded definition see Revenue and Taxation (R&T) Code Section 61, 107-107.9, 480.6 and Property Tax Rules 20,21-22, and 27-28 available online at",
  Achangeinownership: "A change in ownership occurs when a possessory interest is created, assigned, or upon the expiration of the lease per Revenue & Taxation Code Section 61 available online at",
  Baseyearvalue: "Base year value computation is fully explained in Property Tax Rule 21 available online at"
};
