var Welcome = {
  SearchTitle: "Real Estate Toolkit",
  SearchDescription: "The Real Estate Toolkit is designed to be a one-stop shop for real estate professionals.  The site provides extensive information about Assessor programs and services.  The site has information, resources and tools designed for real estate professionals in their own professional development.",
  Welcome: "Welcome!",
  ThisReal: "The Real Estate Toolkit is designed to be a one-stop shop for real estate professionals.  The site provides extensive information about Assessor programs and services.  The site has information, resources and tools designed for real estate professionals in their own professional development.",
  Additionally: "Additionally, this page features information that real eastate professionals may want to share with clients and prospective clients about the opportunities and responsibilities that accompany real estate ownership.  Website users are welcome to 'cut and paste' information to share in newsletters and other marketing vehicles such as email, social media and direct mail.",
  TheOffice: 'The Office also publishes an informative <a href="https://assessor.lacounty.gov/news-information/newsletters">Real Estate Pro Newsletter</a>, a quarterly update on the latest trend and topics in the real estate market, tips fo real estate professionals, and more.',
  Subscribe: "Subscribe to Real Estate Pro Newsletter",
  forquarterly: " for quarterly updates on the latest trends and topics in the real estate market, tips for real estate professionals, and more.",
  Quote: "We greatly value our relationships with Real Estate Professionals, and want to ensure you have access to helpful and timely information from our office. Please feel free to share any information you find here with your clients, and reach out if we can be of service.",
  RequestSpeaker: "Request a Speaker",
  RequestCertificateCommendation: "Request a Certificate Commendation",
  AssessorPrang: "Assessor Prang Began Career as Real Estate Agent",
  LosAngeles: "Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang\u2019s first job right out of college was as a Realtor with RE/MAX in Jeff\u2019s hometown of Warren, Michigan. That experience has proven to be invaluable for Assessor Prang as he continues to work alongside colleagues and friends in the real estate business, which is why he created the Real Estate Tool Kit.",
  Itsalsojust: "It\u2019s also just one of the motivating factors that propelled Assessor Prang to seek and be elected as the 27th Assessor in 2014 and re-elected in 2018 and 2022.",
  AssessorPrangis: "Assessor Prang is a graduate of James Madison College at Michigan State University. After graduation, Mr. Prang relocated to California where he served nearly 18 years as a councilmember for the City of West Hollywood, including four terms as Mayor among many other positions in the public sector.",
  Upontaking: "Upon taking office in 2014 as the Los Angeles County Assessor, Mr. Prang implemented sweeping reforms to ensure that the strictest ethical guidelines rooted in fairness, accuracy and integrity would be adhered to in his office.",
  TheLosAngelesv2: "The Los Angeles County Assessor\u2019s Office is the largest office of its kind in the nation with 1,300 employees and provides the foundation for a property tax system that generates $19 billion annually.",
  AssessorPrangState: "Assessor Prang is a State Board of Equalization licensed appraiser, an Executive Committee member of the California Assessors\u2019 Association (CAA), and an active member of the International Association of Assessing Officers (IAAO) and the Society of Auditor-Appraisers (SAA).",
  AssessorJeff: "Assessor Jeff Prang lives in Baldwin Hills with his husband Ray Vizcarra, who is the instrumental music director at Redondo Union High School."
};
var Prop13 = {
  SearchTitle: "Proposition 13",
  SearchDescription: "Information on Proposition 13",
  Thediscovery: "The discovery and valuation of every property in Los Angeles County is the legal responsibility of the Assessor. Assessing property in order to determine its fair market value is the basis for determining the amount of property tax that is to be paid annually by the property\u2019s owner. ",
  Assessing2: "Assessing the value of property relies upon local housing market conditions and the expertise of professional property appraisers in the Assessor's Office. This value is used to determine the annual tax bill which is sent by the County\u2019s Treasurer-Tax Collector to the property owner.",
  in1978: "In 1978, the California Constitution was amended by the voters to restrict increases of property taxes. Proposition 13 requires assessment of each taxable property based on its fair market value and limits a property owner\u2019s general levy tax to 1 percent of the assessed value. For example, an owner of a home assessed at $420,000 will pay a general levy tax of $4,200.",
  Inaddition: "In addition to the 1 percent general levy tax, the constitution allows for voter-approved indebtedness taxes and direct assessment taxes to pay for services such as schools, water districts, lighting maintenance, weed abatement, emergency services, etc.",
  Priorto: "Prior to 1978, the law required reassessment on an annual basis. Not only was this process expensive, it meant that from one year to the next a homeowner\u2019s property tax could double depending on fluctuations in the local housing market. In order to provide property owners with protection against strong fluctuations, Proposition 13 mandated that a property\u2019s assessed value could not increase by more than 2 percent from one year to the next except in the event of a change in ownership, new construction, the property receives a temporary decline in assessed value (Proposition 8), or the property is designated a restricted historical property (Mills Act).",
  Foranexplanation: "For an explanation on your annual property tax bill, please visit:"
};
var WillsandTrust = {
  SearchTitle: "Wills and Trust Tax Savings",
  SearchDescription: "Information on wills and trusts",
  Revocable: "Revocable Living Trust",
  Arevocable: "A revocable living trust may be used if a property owner wishes to make provisions for the transfer of property upon death but is not currently ready to make an actual transfer of ownership. For a trust to be revocable, the \u201Ctrustor\u201D must reserve the right to terminate the trust and retain all trust property. When property is placed in a revocable living trust, there is no \u201Cchange in ownership,\u201D and thus, no reassessment of the current values.",
  Uponthedeath: "Upon the death of the trustor, the revocable living trust becomes irrevocable. In preparing a revocable living trust, those involved may plan to qualify the real property for the parent-child or another exclusion in order to avoid a \u201Cchange in ownership,\u201D and thus reassessment, upon the death of the trustor.",
  Irrevocable: "Irrevocable Trust",
  Anirrevocable: "An irrevocable trust is used when property is transferred during the lifetime \u2014or upon the death\u2014of the property owner, or when the beneficiary of such a trust is changed. These events will (for property tax purposes) generally trigger a reassessment. However, if the parent-child exception applies, and with proper planning, it is possible to leave property to a child in trust and be relieved of the burden of high property taxes. With an Irrevocable Trust, a property owner can maintain ownership of the property for the duration of the owner\u2019s life.",
  TrustDist: "Trust Distributions ",
  Whenproperty: "When property is left to more than one child, one of these children may want the property and the other may seek money or assets of equal value. If the trust provides only the property to each child equally (each getting a one half interest), it will be necessary for one child to transfer their interest to the other in exchange for an equalizing payment. This would no longer be a parent-child transfer, but would be a sibling-to-sibling transfer, which is not excluded from reassessment\u2014thus, a change in ownership will have occurred and the property will be reassessed. In either case consult with a real estate or estate planning expert for advice before claiming any exclusions."
};
var ApplyforHOX = {
  SearchTitle: "Apply for Homeowners' Exemption",
  SearchDescription: "",
  AssessorsTip: "ASSESSOR'S TIP FOR REAL ESTATE PROFESSIONALS: ",
  presentthe: "present the Homeowners\u2019 Exemption form at closing with all the other paperwork in the packet so homeowner has savings at a time of huge expense.",
  Ifyouown: "If you own a home and it is your principal place of residence on January 1, you may apply for an exemption of $7,000 from your assessed value. ",
  Call: "Call",
  or: "or",
  forforms: "for additional information."
};
var Refinance = {
  SearchTitle: "Refinancing: Will I Get Reassessed?",
  SearchDescription: "Information on refinancing and reassessment",
  Will: "Will refinancing lead to a reassessment? Maybe.",
  Willrefinancing: "Will refinancing lead to a reassessment? Maybe. Often times, the initial transfer effected for the purpose of refinancing, whereby a name is placed on or taken off title, will result in a reassessment of the transferred property, if no exclusion applies to the stand-alone transfer. However, if title to the property is later reconveyed to the original owners, and it can be shown (through tax returns, year-end mortgage interest statements, etc.) that the original owners were financially responsible for the property during the period between the original and reconveyance transfers, the Assessor may reverse the change in ownership reassessment associated with the original transfer and exclude the reconveyance transfer from change in ownership as a perfection of title.",
  Refinancingarrangements: "Refinance arrangements and transactions can take many forms. Below we have included examples of those most commonly encountered by the Assessor\u2019s Ownership Division.",
  AddingaCosigner: "Adding a Cosigner on Title to Refinance:",
  Example: "Example:",
  Jackowns: "Jack owns a piece of property and plans to refinance. Due to Jack\u2019s poor credit, his lender requires a cosigner. Jack adds his sister Jill on title as a tenant in common \u2013 such that they each come to hold title to a 50% interest in the property \u2013 so he can obtain the loan. It is Jack and Jill\u2019s intent that Jack continue to make all payments in connection with the property (mortgage, utilities, etc.). Notwithstanding this private arrangement, adding someone to title is generally indicative of a change in ownership. In this situation, the Assessor will initially view the addition of Jill to title as a 50% change in ownership and reassess the property.",
  However: "However, Jill later transfers legal title back to Jack, such that he again holds title to a 100% interest in the property. If Jack is able to show (through tax returns, year-end mortgage interest statements, etc.) that he alone was financially responsible for the property during the entire time that Jill was on title, the Assessor can (1) reverse the initial change in ownership reassessment, and (2) exclude the reconveyance from change in ownership reassessment as well.",
  Removinganowner: "Removing an Owner from Title to Refinance:",
  JackandJill: "Jack and Jill, brother and sister, own a piece of property as joint tenants (50/50) and plan to refinance. Due to Jack\u2019s poor credit, their lender requires that he be removed from title. Jack transfers title to his 50% joint tenant interest to Jill \u2013 such that she comes to hold title to a 100% interest in the property \u2013 so they can obtain the loan. It is Jack and Jill\u2019s intent that they continue to make equal contributions toward expenses associated with the property (mortgage, utilities, etc.). Notwithstanding this private arrangement, removing someone from title is generally indicative of a change in ownership. In this situation, the Assessor will initially view the removal of Jack from title as a 50% change in ownership and reassess the property.",
  HoweverJill: "However, Jill later transfers title to a 50% interest in the property back to Jack, such that they each again hold title to a 50% interest in the property. If Jack and Jill are able to show (through tax returns, year-end mortgage interest statements, etc.) that they made equal financial contributions toward expenses associated with the property during the entire time that only Jill was on title, the Assessor can (1) reverse the initial change in ownership reassessment, and (2) exclude the reconveyance from change in ownership reassessment as well.",
  AcquiringTitle: "Acquiring Title in the Name of a Third-Party:",
  Jackplans: "Jack plans on purchasing a piece of property, but has insufficient credit to obtain a loan to do so. Instead of attempting to acquire a loan and purchase the property in his own name, his sister Jill, who has good credit, agrees to take title in her name \u2013 provided Jack make all payments in connection with the property (down payment, mortgage, utilities, etc.).",
  LaterJack: "Later, Jack, having built up sufficient credit to obtain a loan on his own, wishes to refinance and take title in his own name. Jill transfers title to a 100% interest in the property to Jack. As the transfer of title from one person to another is generally indicative of a change in ownership, the Assessor may initially view the transfer of title from Jill to Jack as a 100% change in ownership and reassess the property; however, if Jack is able to show (through tax returns, year-end mortgage interest statements, etc.) that he alone was financially responsible for the property during the entire time that Jill was on title, the Assessor can exclude the transfer of title from Jill to Jack as a transfer of bare legal title and not reassess.",
  RemovingPropertyfromtrust: "Removing Property From a Trust to Refinance:",
  JackandJoan: "Jack and Joan, husband and wife, each own a 50% community property interest in a piece of property, via their revocable living trust, and plan to refinance. Their lender requires that title to the property be transferred from the trust to Jack and Joan outright. Jack and Joan, as trustees of their revocable living trust, transfer title to the property to themselves as joint tenants (50/50), so they can obtain the loan. Absent information on the trust, the Assessor may initially view the removal of the property from the trust as a 100% change in ownership and reassess the property; however, if Jack and Joan provide a copy of the trust document showing that they owned the property, as trustors/present beneficiaries of the trust before the transfer, and continue to own the property as individuals afterward, the Assessor can exclude the transfer from change in ownership and need not reassess the property.",
  RemovingPropertyfromlegal: "Removing Property From a Legal Entity to Refinance",
  Apieceof1: "A piece of property is owned by an LLC. The members of the LLC are Jack, Joan, John, and Jill. They each hold a 25% membership interest in the LLC. In order to refinance, the lender requires that title to the property be transferred from the LLC to the members outright. The LLC transfers the property to Jack, Joan, John, and Jill, each as to a 25% interest, so the loan can be acquired. Absent information on the LLC\u2019s composition, the Assessor may initially view the transfer of the property from the LLC as a 100% change in ownership and reassess the property; however, if Jack, Joan, John, and Jill are able to show that they each held a 25% interest in the LLC before the transfer \u2013 by providing copies of the LLC\u2019s operating agreement and tax return for the year in which the transfer occurred (if available) \u2013 the Assessor can exclude the transfer from change in ownership and need not reassess the property.",
  Apieceof2: "A piece of property is owned by an LLC. The members of the LLC are Jack, Joan, John, and Jill. They each hold a 25% membership interest in the LLC. In order to refinance, the lender requires that title to the property be transferred from the LLC to Jack alone, as he is the only member with sufficient credit to qualify for the loan. The LLC transfers title to the property to Jack, so they can obtain the loan. It is the intent of the parties that the LLC \u2013 not Jack in his individual capacity \u2013 continue to make all payments in connection with the property (mortgage, utilities, etc.). Notwithstanding this private arrangement, as a disproportional transfer between a legal entity and individual constitutes a reassessable change in ownership, here, the property will be reassessed.",
  Jacklater: "Jack later transfers title to the property back to LLC. If the LLC is able to show (through tax returns, year-end mortgage interest statements, etc.) that the LLC \u2013 not Jack in his individual capacity \u2013 was financially responsible for the property during the entire time that Jack was on title, the Assessor can (1) reverse the initial change in ownership reassessment, and (2) exclude the reconveyance from change in ownership reassessment as well."
};
var WhenReassessed = {
  SearchTitle: "When Does the Assessor Reappraise Property?",
  SearchDescription: "When do I get reassessed?",
  Change: "Change in Ownership reappraisal",
  ChangeOwnershipDesc: "The Assessor receives copies of deeds and other recorded documents from the Clerk-Recorder\u2019s Office on a continuous basis. These documents are reviewed carefully to determine if a reappraisal is required under State law. If it is required, an appraisal is made to determine the fair market value of the property as of the date of transfer.",
  NewConstruction: "New Construction Reappraisal",
  NewConstdesc1: "The Assessor receives copies of building permits from municipal and county agencies on a continuous basis. These permits are reviewed carefully to determine if a reappraisal is required under State law. If it is required, an appraisal is made to determine the fair market value of the newly constructed portion as of the date the construction is completed.",
  NewConstdesc2: "If the new construction project is only partially completed on the January 1st lien date, the law requires the Assessor to estimate the fair market value in its current state of completion, until the project is 100% complete and the final assessment is determined. If, for example, a building permit is issued on September 15th and on the subsequent January 1st construction is only 40% completed, the Assessor will estimate the value of the \u201C40% portion\u201D and add that value to the next assessment roll.",
  DeclineinValue: "Decline in Market Value",
  DIV1: "The Assessor\u2019s Office is required to lower the assessment of real property to the lesser of its current market value or the factored Proposition 13 base year value as of January 1 of each year.",
  DIV2: "The Assessor makes every effort to consider declines in value and to assess all property at the lesser of market value or its factored base year value each year without the need for individual owners making an informal request or filing a formal appeal. Informal requests made by taxpayer are reviewed by our appraisers and can be filed by the property owner or their agent. Lowered values are temporary for one year, until the following lien date (January 1).",
  DisasterRelief: "Disaster Relief",
  Disasterdesc: "If a sudden and major calamity such as fire or flooding damages or destroys your property, you may be eligible for property tax relief. In such cases, the Assessor\u2019s Office will immediately reappraise the property to reflect its damaged condition. In addition, when you rebuild it in a like or similar manner, the property will retain its previous value for tax purposes."
};
var Inter_Spousal = {
  SearchTitle: "Inter-Spousal",
  SearchDescription: "The inter-spousal exclusion protects the surviving spouse when a death occurs or when a spouse retains property in a divorce settlement from reassessment. The definition of married couples includes both same sex couples as well as opposite sex couples. ",
  Theinter: "The inter-spousal exclusion protects the surviving spouse when a death occurs or when a spouse retains property in a divorce settlement from reassessment. The definition of married couples includes both same sex couples as well as opposite sex couples. ",
  Thereisno: "There is no claim form required to be filed to establish the inter-spousal exclusion.\u202F However, supporting documentation, such as a divorce decree, settlement agreement, or death certificate may be requested by the Assessor to establish the eligibility of the exclusion. ",
  FromJune: "From June 16, 2008 to November 5, 2008, same sex marriages performed during this period are recognized. On June 28, 2013, same sex marriages are recognized. ",
  FilingReq: "Filing Requirements: ",
  Whenrecording: "When recording the Grant Deed, Quitclaim deed, Affidavit of Death of Joint Tenant or Affidavit of Death of Trustee, complete the Preliminary Change of Ownership report, section A which provides additional information to the <strong>assessor</strong> for the type of transfer. "
};
var JointTenancy = {
  SearchTitle: "Joint Tenancy",
  SearchDescription: "Ownership can take several forms in which property owners can hold vesting title to their real property.\u202F One of these forms of holding title is Joint Tenants. ",
  Ownership: "Ownership can take several forms in which property owners can hold vesting title to their real property.\u202F One of these forms of holding title is Joint Tenants. ",
  Priorto: "Prior to the predominance of family trusts, families used joint tenancy as a form of estate planning to transfer the real property from one generation to the next without triggering a reassessment to protect the Proposition 13 factored base year value. ",
  JointTenancies: "Joint Tenancies are co-ownership interest in real property. A Joint Tenancy must include these four unities: ",
  UnityInterest: "Unity of interest: The interest of each owner is equal. ",
  UnityTime: "Unity of time: The interest of the owners is acquired at the same time. ",
  UnityPossession: "Unity of possession: The owners have the right of survivorship. ",
  UnityTitle: "Unity of title: The document must specify a joint tenancy vesting. (If a vesting is not specified, it is presumed to be a tenancy in common.) ",
  Whenjoint: "When joint tenants acquire a real property, there is a change in ownership unless there are applicable exclusions. ",
  Jointtenants: "Joint tenants who add another joint tenant to the ownership of the property, does not trigger a reassessment because the original joint tenants remain on title. This is a creation of joint tenancy where the original transferors are still on title. The added joint tenant is the other than original transferor. ",
  Whenthe: "When the other than original transferor is removed from the joint tenancy, this is a reversion back to the original transferors. This reversion does not trigger a reassessment. ",
  Jointtenancy: "Joint tenancy is a form of ownership where individual co-owner real property. Therefore, a trust or a legal entity cannot be joint tenants or joint tenants with individuals. The vesting can only be tenants in common. ",
  FilingReq: "Filing Requirements: ",
  Whenrecording: "When recording the Grant Deed, Quitclaim deed, Affidavit of Death of Joint Tenant or Affidavit of Death of Trustee, complete the Preliminary Change of Ownership report, section A which provides additional information to the Assessor for the type of transfer. "
};
var Events = {
  SearchTitle: "Events",
  SearchDescription: "Upcoming events",
  TheLosAngeles2: "The Los Angeles County Assessor's Office and related agencies offer many educational seminars throughout the year, free of charge to the public. View the sessions currently being offered below.",
  Comebacksoon2: "Come back soon for more events by the Assessor's Office",
  "2021Taxpayers": "2021 Taxpayers' Bill of Rights Hearing",
  TheCAboard: 'The CA Board of Equalization will be hosting the annual Taypayers\u2019 Bill of Rights hearing before the Board Members via WebEx on Tuesday, August 24th at approximately 10am. This public hearing will provide members of the public the opportunity to provide comments on items discussed in the annual <a href="https://boe.ca.gov/pdf/pub316.pdf" target="_blank">BOE Taxpayers\u2019 Rights Advocate\u2019s Annual Report</a>, as well as present ideas, concerns, and input on the quality of agency service related to the BOE\u2019s administration of its programs.',
  Sept132021: '<h4>SEPTEMBER 13, 2021, 6:30PM</h4><h5>SONC VIRTUAL BOARD MEETING</h5> <p>Join Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and the Sherman Oaks Neighborhood Council for the virtual Board Meeting on Monday, September 13, 2021, at 6:30 PM. The Assessor will be providing updates on Prop. 19, available property tax savings for property owners, and much more.</p> <p>Visit the <a href="https://www.shermanoaksnc.org/" target="_blank">Sherman Oaks Neighborhood Council</a> website for more information and an updated Zoom link.</p>',
  Sept172021: '<h4>SEPTEMBER 17, 2021, 7:00PM</h4><h5>GLENDORA CITY COUNCIL MEETING</h5> <p>Join Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and the Glendora City Council on Tuesday, September 14, 2021, at 7:00 PM for the regular Council meeting. The Assessor will be providing updates on Prop. 19, available property tax savings for property owners, and market data information for Glendora and the surrounding area.</p> <p>Visit the <a href="http://cityofglendora.org/home" target="_blank">City of Glendora</a> website for more information and an updated Zoom link.</p>',
  Sept282021: '<h4>SEPTEMBER 28, 2021, 6:30PM</h4><h5>LA MIRADA CITY COUNCIL</h5> <p>Join Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and the La Mirada City Council on Tuesday, September 28, 2021, at 6:30 PM at La Mirada City Hall. The Assessor will be providing updates on Prop. 19, available property tax savings for property owners, and market data insights, and much more.</p> <p>Visit the <a href="https://www.cityoflamirada.org/about-us/city-council" target="_blank">La Mirada City Council</a> website for more information and an Council agenda.</p>',
  Oct132021: `<h4>October 13, 2021, 11:30 PM - 1:30 PM</h4><h5>POMONA CHAMBER NETWORKING AT NOON</h5> <p>Join Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and the Pomona Chamber on Wednesday October 13, 2021, between 11:30 AM - 1:30 PM at Metro Ale House. If your business was impacted by the pandemic and has not received a reduction of value, come learn more about the Assessor's Office and how to submit a Decline-in-Value application.</p> <p>$20 - Members | $30 - Nonmembers</p> <p>Register online at the <a href="https://pomonachamber.org/event/networking-at-noon-luncheon-2021-10-13/" target="_blank">Pomona Chamber</a> website. For more information, contact (909) 622-1256 or email <a href="mailto:info@pomonachamber.org">info@pomonachamber.org</a>.</p>`,
  Dec82021v3: '<h4>DECEMBER 8, 2021, 1:00PM</h4><h5>PROPOSITION 19 TOWN HALL</h5> <p>Proposition 19 made significant changes to the property tax administration system. Our Proposition 19 Town Hall is scheduled to solicit feedback from the public and real estate practitioners about the issues, concerns, deficiencies and confusion that have occurred during the implementation of <a href="http://assessor.lacounty.gov/prop19" target="_blank">Proposition 19</a>.</p>',
  Registernow: "Register Today!",
  Register: "Register Now!",
  WatchVid: "Watch Video",
  Mar232022: "<h4>MARCH 23, 2022, 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR'S ADU RESOURCE SEMINAR</h5> <p>We will be joined by Southern California Edison and the Los Angeles County Departments of Regional Planning and Public Works to discuss some of the issues property owners need to know when adding an ADU to their property.  We highly encourage everyone to submit questions ahead of time.</p>",
  Mar292022: '<h4>MARCH 29 - 31, 2022, 12PM - 1PM</h4><h5>VIRTUAL BUSINESS PERSONAL PROPERTY STATEMENT - Q&A SESSION</h5> <p>Please join us to receive help on filing a 571-L form and answers to other commonly asked questions. We highly encourage you to watch the "How to Prepare a Business Personal Property Statement, Form 571-L webinar".</p>',
  May212022: "<h4>May 21, 2022, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR'S 2022 HOMEOWNERS RESOURCE FAIR</h5><p>Please join us at the Huntington Park Community Center. The fair will cover Accessory Dwelling Units and New Construction, Parent-Child Transfers (Prop. 19), Property Tax Savings Programs, and more. It's free and open to the public.</p>",
  Clicktoview: "Click to view English and Spanish flyer",
  ClicktoviewEN: "Click to view the flyer",
  ClicktoviewCN: "Click to view the flyer in English and Chinese",
  ClicktoviewENSPCN: "Click to view the flyer in English, Chinese, and Spanish",
  ClicktoviewENSPCNAR: "Click to view the flyer in English, Chinese, Spanish, and Armenian",
  ClickInvitation: "Click to view invitiation",
  PastEvents: "Past Events",
  Oct282022: "<h4>OCTOBER 28, 2022, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR'S FALL HOMEOWNERS' RESOURCE FAIR</h5> <p>Please join us at the Hacienda Heights Community & Recreation Center. The fair will cover Accessory Dwelling Units and New Construction, Parent-Child Transfers (Prop. 19), Property Tax Savings Programs, and more. It\u2019s free and open to the public!</p>",
  Dec82022: "<h4>CEREMONIAL MATTERS: <BR>DECEMBER 8, 2022, 11:30AM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR PRANG'S OATH OF OFFICE CEREMONY</h5> <p>Please join us for Assessor Prang's Oath of Office Ceremony at Monterey Park City Hall:</p><strong>Monterey Park City Hall</strong><br>City Council Chambers<br>320 West Newmark Avenue<br>Monterey Park, CA 91754",
  RSVP: "RSVP Today!",
  Dec122022: "<h4>DECEMBER 12, 2022, 11AM</h4><h5>HYBRID CALIFORNIA COUNTY ASSESSORS' INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY AUTHORITY BOARD MEETING</h5> <p>3636 American River Drive, Suite 200 <br>Sacramento, CA 95864-5952</p>",
  ClickAgenda: "Click to view agenda",
  JoinZoom: "Join Zoom Meeting",
  Mar42023: "<h4>MARCH 4, 2023, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S HOMEOWNERS' RESOURCE FAIR</h5> <p>Please join us at the Live Oak Park Community Center in Temple City. The fair will cover Parent-Child Transfers (Prop. 19), Property Tax Savings, Business Property Statements, ADUs, and more. It\u2019s free and open to the public!</p>",
  "Mar302023-571L": '<h4>MARCH 30, 2023, 12PM-1PM</h4><h5>VIRTUAL BUSINESS PERSONAL PROPERTY STATEMENT \u2014 Q&A SESSION</h5> <p>Please join us to receive help on filing a 571-L form and answers to other commonly asked questions. We highly encourage you to watch the "How to Prepare a Business Personal Property Statement, Form 571-L webinar".</p>',
  "Oct142023-HORF": "<h4>OCTOBER 14, 2023, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S HOMEOWNERS' RESOURCE FAIR</h5> <p>Please join us at the San Fernando Recreation Park. The fair will cover Parent-Child Transfers (Prop. 19), Property Tax Savings Programs, Death of a Property Owner, ADUs and more. It\u2019s free and open to the public!</p>",
  Jan202024: "<h4>JANUARY 20, 2024, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S HOMEOWNERS' RESOURCE FAIR IN SANTA CLARITA</h5> <p>Please join us at the Canyon Country Community Center. The fair will cover Parent-Child Transfers (Prop. 19), Property Tax Savings Programs, Death of a Property Owner, Misfortune and Calamity, Tax Relief, and more. It\u2019s free and open to the public!</p>",
  Mar282024: "<h4>March 28, 2024, 12PM - 1PM</h4><h5>Virtual Business Personal Property Statement Webinar and Q&A Session</h5> <p>Join the Los Angeles County Assessor's Office for a virtual Business Personal Property Statement Webinar and Q&A session.</p><p><strong>Topics include:</strong><ul><li>Why did I receive a business property statement (571-L)?</li><li>Do I have to file a 571-L?</li><li>Step-by-step assistance about how to file</li><li>Commonly asked questions</li></ul>Zoom breakout rooms will be available in English, Chinese, Russian, Spanish, Farsi, and Armenian.</p> ",
  Apr92024: "<h4>April 9, 2024, 6:30PM - 8:30PM</h4><h5>Assessor's Boat & Vessel Owners Town Hall</h5> <p>Please join us at Burton Chase Park in Marina del Rey. The town hall will cover Vessel Property Statements, Vessel Assessment, Vessel Ownership Verification, Vessel Registration (DMV or Coast Guard), and Additional Vessel Resources. It's free and open to the public!</p>",
  Nov92024: "<h4>November 9, 2024, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S HOMEOWNERS' RESOURCE FAIR IN TORRANCE</h5> <p>Please join us at the Torrance Cultural Arts Center. The fair will cover Parent-child Transfers (Prop. 19), Death of a Property Owner, Misfortune & Calamity, Disabled Veterans' Exemption, and much more. It's free and open to the public!</p>",
  Mar42025: "<h4>MARCH 4, 2025, 12PM - 1PM</h4><h5>VIRTUAL BUSINESS PERSONAL PROPERTY STATEMENT WEBINAR AND Q&A SESSION</h5> <p>Join the Los Angeles County Assessor's Office for a virtual Business Personal Property Statement Webinar and Q&A session.</p><p>Topics include:<br><ul><li>Why did I receive a business property statement (571-L)?</li><li>Do I have to file a 571-L?</li><li>Step-by-step assistance about how to file</li><li>Commonly asked questions</li></ul>",
  Mar62025: "<h4>MARCH 6, 2025, 12PM - 1PM</h4><h5>VIRTUAL BUSINESS PERSONAL PROPERTY STATEMENT WEBINAR AND Q&A SESSION</h5> <p>Join the Los Angeles County Assessor's Office for a virtual Business Personal Property Statement Webinar and Q&A session.</p><p>Topics include:<br><ul><li>Why did I receive a business property statement (571-L)?</li><li>Do I have to file a 571-L?</li><li>Step-by-step assistance about how to file</li><li>Commonly asked questions</li></ul>",
  Mar132025v3: "<h4>MARCH 13, 2025, 6pm - 8pm</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S DISASTER RELIEF WEBINAR</h5> <p>Join the Los Angeles County Assessor's Office along with representatives from the Treasurer and Tax Collector, Auditor-Controller, and Regional Planning for a Disaster Relief Webinar and Q&A session.</p><p>Topics include:<br><ul><li>Property Tax Relief</li><li>Online Resources</li><li>Live Question and Answer Session</li></ul>",
  Mar222025: "<h4>MARCH 22, 2025, 9AM - 12PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR\u2019S DISASTER RELIEF RESOURCE FAIR IN DUARTE</h5> <p>Please join us at the Duarte Community Center. The fair is an opportunity for property owners impacted by the Eaton and Palisades Fires to hear direct updates concerning property tax relief from Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and other County departments. Attendees will have the opportunity to direct questions to subject matter experts and visit with participating community partners.</p><p><strong>Duarte Community Center</strong><br>1600 Huntington Drive<br>Duarte, CA 91010</p>",
  ZoomRegLink: "Zoom Registration Link",
  Regfor: "Register for",
  Apr272025: "<h4>April 27, 2025, 9am - 12pm</h4><h5>Westside Disaster Relief Resource Fair in Los Angeles</h5> <p>Please join us at the Los Angeles Police Facility. The fair is an opportunity for property owners impacted by the Eaton and Palisades Fires to hear direct updates concerning property tax relief from Los Angeles County Assessor Jeff Prang and other County and City departments. Attendees will have the opportunity to direct questions to subject matter experts and visit with participating community partners.</p><p><strong>Los Angeles Airport Police Facility</strong> <br>9160 Loyola Blvd <br>Los Angeles, CA 90045</p>",
  Apr12026v2: "<h4>APRIL 15, 2026, 11AM - 12PM</h4><h5>BUSINESS PERSONAL PROPERTY STATEMENT FILING WEBINAR</h5> <p>Find out what you need to know about filing the 571-L form for your Business Personal Property.</p><p><strong>Topics include:</strong></p><ul><li>What do I need to report?</li><li>How do I submit my 571-L?</li><li>Do l even need to file?</li><li>Q & A Session</li></ul>",
  May052026: "<h4>MAY 13, 2026, 6PM - 8PM</h4><h5>ASSESSOR'S BOAT & VESSEL OWNERS' TOWN HALL</h5> <p>Join Assessor Jeff Prang and Assessor's Office experts to learn about Vessel Property Statements, Vessel Assessments, Vessel Ownership Verification, Vessel Registration, additional resources and much more!</p><p><strong>Long Beach Yacht Club</strong><br>6201 Appian Way<br>Long Beach, CA 90803</p>"
};
